From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Bert Belder <bertbelder@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 18 Oct 2026 12:00:00 +0000
Subject: Let an embedder relocate the run queue out of its events reach

The run queue runs through links the loop keeps in its own memory, and the
loop walks and mutates that list itself: arming an event takes a link off
the loop's free list and splices it in, disarming unlinks one and returns
it, and head() walks from the loop's head link. An embedder that runs the
loop's work under memory isolation, where a unit of work may address only
its own objects, cannot leave that memory where the work can write it: the
list the host walks in turn() would then run through links a unit chose.

Such an embedder installs EventQueueHooks, which take over every queue
operation -- arming, disarming, the head and would-sleep walks, the
depth-first reset turn() does around a fire(), and the loop's setup and
teardown -- so the loop keeps the queue wherever the embedder puts it,
out of the work's reach, and the work journals its arms and disarms for
the embedder to apply rather than writing the list directly. With no hooks
installed, which is the default, the loop uses its own inline queue exactly
as before, so a build that installs none is unaffected.

An armed event still keeps eight bytes naming its place. With no hooks they
hold the EventLink* the loop gave it; with hooks the loop treats them as
opaque and the embedder stores what names the place, keeping the loop's
`link == nullptr` test for "not armed" (a zero value is unarmed either way).
The event's size is unchanged. The loop names its relocated queue with a
handle rather than a pointer, so that a handle a unit of work forges in the
loop resolves, through the embedder's table, to no queue at all rather than
to memory the unit chose.
